        "role": "user",
        "content": "You are coding a transcript from a decision-making experiment. Three participants discussed which of three job applicants (A, B, C) is best. Each privately held three of five ratings (positive or negative) per applicant.\n\nParticipant 3 privately held exactly these ratings:\nApplicant A: experience: positive; intelligence: negative; motivation: negative\nApplicant B: interpersonal skills: positive; personal skills: negative; experience: positive\nApplicant C: interpersonal skills: positive; intelligence: positive; motivation: negative\n\nBelow are all public messages by participant 3.\n\nRound 1: My information confirms A’s intelligence is negative (not missing), along with positive experience and negative motivation. Combining reports, A has two positives and two negatives among the four known traits; personal skills are unknown, so A’s strength is 4 if positive or 1 if negative. B is fully known at 3 positives and 2 negatives (strength 4). C is fully known at 2 positives and 3 negatives (strength 1). Thus B is best unless A’s personal skills are positive, in which case A ties B.\nRound 2: Agreed: with A’s personal skills negative, A has two positives and three negatives (strength 1). B has three positives and two negatives (strength 4), while C has two positives and three negatives (strength 1). B is therefore the unique best applicant.\nRound 3: Agreed. B is uniquely strongest at 4; A and C are tied at 1 based on the complete profiles.\nRound 4: Agreed: B is the unique best applicant with strength 4, while A and C each have strength 1. B should be ranked first.\nRound 5: The full information confirms B is the unique best applicant with strength 4; A and C each have strength 1. I’ll rank B first.\n\nFor each of participant 3's own ratings, code what the public messages said about it:\n- \"shared\": some message states this applicant's rating on this characteristic correctly, and no message contradicts it.\n- \"misstated\": some message gives the opposite rating for this applicant and characteristic, or describes it as unknown or missing although the participant held it.\n- \"withheld\": no message states this specific rating. Totals or counts that do not name the characteristic (\"A has two positives\") count as withheld.\nCode only what participant 3 said, not what other participants said."
      }
